## Migration: `EventProcessor` → .NET 8

### Problems with the original code on .NET 6+

| API | Issue |
|-----|-------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — will crash at runtime |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable for waking `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be interrupted cooperatively |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls before `Dequeue` → only one item processed per wake |

---

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Wait for signal OR cancellation — replaces bare WaitOne + Interrupt
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle });

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            // Drain all queued items (fixes original race condition)
            while (true)
            {
                string? item;
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_queue.Count == 0)
                        break;
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
                }
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();              // signals the token → wakes WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);        // wait up to 5s for clean exit
        // No Thread.Abort needed — the loop exits cooperatively
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

### Key changes explained

1. **`CancellationTokenSource` replaces both `Interrupt` and `Abort`** — calling `Cancel()` sets the token, which wakes `WaitHandle.WaitAny` immediately.

2. **`WaitHandle.WaitAny(event, token.WaitHandle)`** — the thread wakes on *either* a new event or cancellation, eliminating the need for forced interruption.

3. **Loop drains the full queue** — the original code had a race: multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` would leave items stuck. The inner `while` loop fixes this.

4. **`IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` and `ManualResetEventSlim` are disposable; leaking them causes timer/callback leaks.

---

### Common pitfalls to watch for

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Forgetting to check `IsCancellationRequested` after `WaitAny` | You'll process stale items after cancellation |
| Not draining the queue | Same bug as original — items lost between Set/Reset |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks internal timer handles |
| Expecting instant stop without `Join` | Cooperative cancellation only takes effect at the next checkpoint |
| Using `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` in the loop body | Fine for task-based code, but in a raw `Thread` it surfaces as unhandled `OperationCanceledException` — prefer the `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` pattern here |

---

### Even simpler: use `Channel<T>` (recommended for new code)

If you're willing to go fully modern, `System.Threading.Channels` eliminates the manual synchronization entirely:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _worker;

    public void Start()
    {
        _worker = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // timeout fallback
        if (_worker != null)
            await _worker.ConfigureAwait(false);
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();
    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

This version has no locks, no manual events, and cancellation is fully integrated via `ReadAllAsync(token)`.
